    "q-95405": {
      "dom_id": "step-q95405",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Given that $A$ is an $8\\times 8$ real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable\n\nII. $A$ must have $8$ real and distinct eigenvalues\n\nIII. If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors that correspond to different eigenvalues, then $u⊥v$",
      "worked_solution": "Given that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $R$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the spectral theorem, the eigenvalues are real but not necessarily distinct. This occurs when the characteristic polynomial has roots whose (algebraic) multiplicity is greater than $1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues must be orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
